  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nThrough next Tuesday...Noticeably drier air that is \"not so hot\" \ncontinues to filter into the region, and this will remain across \nthe region into at early next week. High pressure aloft and at the\nsurface will also assert itself into the region through the \nbeginning of the weekend. Rain chances will essentially be nil \nacross the area during this time. High temperatures each day will \nbe a bit less than they've been in recent weeks, as they range \nfrom the upper 80s to low 90s Thursday through Saturday. Some \ngradual warming will then ensue Sunday through Tuesday, as highs \npeak in the low and middle 90s. The result of the frontal \nboundary will continue to slowly sink south through the forecast \narea today. During the afternoon hours today, as highs warm into \nthe middle 90s across mainly southern portions of the CWA, a stray\nshower or storm can't be ruled out in the vicinity of this \nboundary. The remainder of the forecast area will remain dry as \nhighs warm into the low and middle 90s. e drier airmass across the\nregion will especially be felt during the night time hours as \nlows fall into the 60s.\n\nOnce again, rain chances will remain tough to come by during most of \nthis forecast period.  Ridging aloft will weaken enough on Sunday, \nallowing a disturbance embedded in northwest flow to traverse the \nCWA.  This will bring some isolated showers to the forecast area \nSunday.  Then late Monday into Tuesday, a shortwave dropping \nsoutheast into the region will yield another chance for rain \nacross the forecast area Tuesday into Tuesday night. A greater\nchance for showers and storms associated with a more significant\nfrontal system could impact the area just beyond this forecast,\nand this will be something to monitor for as we get closer in\ntime. /19/EC/",
